INT. STATE SUPREME COURT - APPELLATE CHAMBER - DAY

Marble, mahogany, a high bench. Cameras off. A single pitcher of water sweats at the lectern.

CHIEF JUSTICE OKSANA PETRENKO, 60s, white hair pinned tight, sits alone on the bench with a pen balanced between two fingers. At the lectern, DESMOND CARUTHERS, 50s, rumpled suit, sweats through his notes. At the state's table, NADINE ASHWORTH-KIM, 40s, charcoal suit, a thick binder squared before her.

                    DESMOND
Your Honor, the statute is plain.

                    OKSANA
Plain to whom, Mr. Caruthers?

                    DESMOND
To any reasonable reader.

                    OKSANA
Are you reasonable?

                    DESMOND
I try to be.

                    OKSANA
That's not an answer.

                    DESMOND
It's the only honest one I have.

She almost smiles. Taps the pen on the bench.

                    OKSANA
Take me through section four.

                    DESMOND
Section four governs notice.

                    OKSANA
And your client?

                    DESMOND
Received none.

                    OKSANA
Not even a letter?

                    DESMOND
A letter was mailed. It was never delivered.

                    OKSANA
Mailed to where?

                    DESMOND
An address where my client hadn't lived in six years.

                    OKSANA
Whose fault was that?

                    DESMOND
The county's. They had the forwarding card.

Nadine's pen stops moving. Oksana sees it.

                    OKSANA
Ms. Ashworth-Kim. You have something to say?

                    NADINE
Not yet.

                    OKSANA
Then Mr. Caruthers continues.

                    DESMOND
Thank you. Your Honor, if the court allows this, thousands of cases reopen.

                    OKSANA
I'm aware of that.

                    DESMOND
And I'd ask the court to consider whether thousands of cases were ever properly closed.

A silence. Oksana sets down her pen.

                    OKSANA
That's a bold sentence.

                    DESMOND
It's a true one.

                    OKSANA
Sit down, Mr. Caruthers.

He gathers his papers and retreats. Nadine rises, walks to the lectern, and opens her binder. She does not look at it.

                    NADINE
May it please the court.

                    OKSANA
It might. Go ahead.

                    NADINE
The forwarding card was misfiled.

                    OKSANA
By whom?

                    NADINE
By a clerk who no longer works for the county.

                    OKSANA
That's not an excuse.

                    NADINE
It's not meant as one. It's an explanation.

                    OKSANA
They sound similar from up here.

                    NADINE
They feel different from down here.

Oksana's eyebrow lifts. Desmond, at his table, almost laughs and then stops himself.

                    NADINE (CONT'D)
The state asks the court to apply the harmless error doctrine.

                    DESMOND
^
It was not harmless!

                    NADINE
^
It was administrative!

                    OKSANA
Both of you.

They stop. The room is very quiet.

                    OKSANA (CONT'D)
Mr. Caruthers, your client lost his house.

                    DESMOND
Yes.

                    OKSANA
Ms. Ashworth-Kim. He lost his house.

Nadine's hand tightens on the lectern. For a moment her composure slips.

                    NADINE
(quietly)
Yes. He did.

Oksana lowers her glasses. Looks at one, then the other.

                    OKSANA
We'll take it under advisement.

She raps once with her pen and rises. The two lawyers rise with her, and for a moment, across the length of the room, they look at each other like the last two people in a lifeboat.
